  {
   "heading": "Your age: avoir + ans",
   "body": "French uses *avoir* for age — literally \"I have twenty years\": *J'ai vingt ans.* (I'm twenty.) To ask: *Tu as quel âge ?* (informal) or *Vous avez quel âge ?* (polite).\n\nNever leave out *ans*: *J'ai trente ans*, not ~~*J'ai trente*~~."
  },
  {
   "heading": "Numbers 0 to 20",
   "body": "*zéro, un, deux, trois, quatre, cinq, six, sept, huit, neuf, dix, onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize, dix-sept, dix-huit, dix-neuf, vingt.*\n\nSome final consonants of numbers are pronounced on their own but change before a noun: *six* /sis/, but *six ans* /si.z‿ɑ̃/; *dix* /dis/, but *dix ans* /di.z‿ɑ̃/; *neuf ans* /nœ.v‿ɑ̃/."
  },
  {
   "heading": "Numbers 21 to 69",
   "body": "The tens are *vingt* (20), *trente* (30), *quarante* (40), *cinquante* (50), *soixante* (60). Add the units with a hyphen — *vingt-deux, trente-cinq* — except for one, which takes *et*: *vingt et un, trente et un, quarante et un*.\n\nPhone numbers are said in pairs: 06 12 45 30 18 → *zéro six, douze, quarante-cinq, trente, dix-huit*."
  },
  {
   "heading": "Personal details",
   "body": "On a form (*un formulaire*) you'll see *nom* (surname), *prénom* (first name), *âge*, *adresse*, *numéro de téléphone* and *nationalité*. To ask for a phone number: *Quel est ton numéro ?* / *Quel est votre numéro de téléphone ?*"
  }
 ],
